{title: When I Survey the Wondrous Cross}
{artist: Isaac Watts}
{key: Eb}
{time: 4/4}
{tempo: 72}

Verse 1
When [Eb]I sur[Ab]vey the [Eb]wondrous [Bb]cross
on [Eb]which the [Ab]Prince of [Bb]glory [Eb]died,
my [Bb]richest [Eb]gain I [Ab]count but [Eb]loss,
and [Ab]pour con[Eb]tempt on [Bb]all my [Eb]pride.

Verse 2
Forbid [Eb]it, Lord, that [Eb]I [Bb]should
boast, [Eb]Save in [Ab]the death [Bb]of [Eb]Christ
my [Bb]God! [Eb]All the vain [Ab]things that [Eb]charm
me [Ab]most, I sacrifice them [Bb]to His [Eb]blood.

Verse 3
See [Eb]from His head, His [Eb]hands, [Bb]His
feet, [Eb]Sorrow and [Ab]love flow [Bb]mingled [Eb]down!
Did [Bb]e'er [Eb]such love [Ab]and sorrow [Eb]meet,
Or [Ab]thorns compose so [Bb]rich a [Eb]crown?

Verse 4
His [Eb]dying crimson, like [Eb]a [Bb]robe,
Spreads [Eb]o'er His [Ab]body on [Bb]the [Eb]tree;
Then [Bb]I [Eb]am dead to [Ab]all the [Eb]globe,
And [Ab]all the globe is [Bb]dead to [Eb]me.

Verse 5
Were [Eb]the whole realm [Eb]of [Bb]nature
mine, [Eb]That were [Ab]a [Bb]present [Eb]far
too [Bb]small; [Eb]Love so [Ab]amazing, so [Eb]divine,
Demands [Ab]my soul, my [Bb]life, my [Eb]all.
